# Documentation style

## Every doc declares its authority

The first thing in any doc (after the title) is the authority statement:

````markdown
# Title

> **Source of truth for:** [what this doc decides]
> **NOT the source of truth for:** [redirect to where that lives]
````

If a doc cannot honestly declare what it decides, that doc probably
shouldn't exist as a separate file.

## Link, don't duplicate

If a section appears in two docs, one must be a link to the other.

````markdown
# ❌ Bad
Risk tiers are R1, R2, R3, R4. R1 is reversible actions like reading...
[content duplicated from concepts/risk-tiers.md]

# ✅ Good
For canonical definitions of risk tiers, see
[concepts/risk-tiers.md](./concepts/risk-tiers.md).
This doc focuses on how Layer 1 applies them.
````

## Use relative links

All cross-doc links are relative paths. This keeps the repo portable and
GitHub-renderable. Never link to GitHub URLs from within the repo.

## Structure conventions

- Headings: ATX-style (`#`, `##`, `###`). No setext (`====`).
- Code blocks: always with language tag (`typescript`, `vue`, `yaml`, `bash`, etc.).
- Tables: always with header separators. Align columns for readability.
- Lists: prefer `-` over `*` for unordered; `1.` for ordered.

## Code examples

Real, executable code. No `// pseudo-code` shortcuts in docs that pretend
to be code.

````markdown
# ❌ Bad
```typescript
// pseudo-code
firewall.protect(agent);
```

# ✅ Good
```typescript
import { Firewall } from '@agent-firewall/core';
const firewall = new Firewall({ policies: './firewall.yml' });
const protectedTools = firewall.wrap([gmailTool]);
```
````

## Language

- English. Always English.
- Active voice over passive.
- Direct over hedged. "The firewall blocks X" not "X may be blocked by the
  firewall depending on configuration".
- Second person ("you") when addressing the developer-reader. First person
  plural ("we") sparingly, only when describing project decisions.

## When to create a new doc vs extend existing

Create a new doc when:
- The topic has a clear, isolated authority scope
- It will be referenced from multiple places
- A future contributor will plausibly look for it under that name

Extend an existing doc when:
- The information naturally belongs to an existing scope
- The new content is < 200 lines
- Creating a new doc would create cross-link spaghetti

## ADRs are not docs

Architectural Decision Records are a different artifact. They capture a
decision at a moment in time and rarely change. They are immutable once
accepted. If a decision changes, write a new ADR that supersedes the old one.

Docs in `docs/*.md` are living documents that evolve with the product.
